      "source": [
        "# Video Generation Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` simulates the core spatiotemporal DiT idea: patchify a small synthetic video, add a per-patch position embedding, and denoise the whole sequence with a transformer-style attention over patches. No numpy; pure Python. We show that temporal coherence emerges even in 1-D when adjacent-frame patches share a denoiser and position embeddings."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: patchify a synthetic 1-D \"video\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def make_video(T_frames=8, rng=None):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    # a \"video\" is a sequence of 1-D values following a smooth trajectory"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    base = rng.gauss(0, 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return [base + 0.3 * t + rng.gauss(0, 0.1) for t in range(T_frames)]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: position embedding per frame"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def pos_embed(t, dim):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return sinusoidal(t, dim)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: denoiser sees the whole sequence"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Instead of denoising each frame independently, our tiny net concatenates all frame values + their position embeddings and predicts the noise for all frames jointly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: temporal coherence test"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "After training, sample a video. Measure the frame-to-frame delta. If the model has learned temporal structure, the deltas stay smaller than sampling each frame independently."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Easy.** In `code/main.py`, compare frame-to-frame delta for (a) independent per-frame sampling, (b) joint sequence sampling. Report the mean and variance of the deltas.\n2. **Medium.** Add a first-frame condition: pin frame 0 to a given value and sample the rest. Measure how the pinned value propagates.\n3. **Hard.** Use HuggingFace diffusers to run CogVideoX-2B on a local GPU. Time 20 inference steps at 720p for a 6-second clip. Profile the spatiotemporal attention to identify the bottleneck."
      ]
    }
